var e=`# CHAPTER 18 — The Pistol-Whip

I heard the blow and both my knees touched the ground.

And, at the same instant, Kim fell beside me. The back of his head bleeding.

I turned my neck with what was left of me and saw who'd done it.

It was my right-hand man. The lieutenant I trusted more than anybody. The one who was going to take over the neighborhood with me gone, split with a partner.

He still had the revolver raised, holding it by the barrel. He'd pistol-whipped the kid in the back of the head.

---

— Why'd you get in my fight? — My voice came out half there. — You know nobody gets in my fights.

— You losing your mind, Alan? — He was white as a sheet. — Weren't you about to lose this one? That kid was gonna kill you today. That kid was gonna kill you for real.

— This isn't how I wanted it to end.

— Doesn't matter how you wanted it. — He crouched down and grabbed me by the arm. — You were gonna die. And if you die, the Banca would never forgive it. I couldn't let that happen. Your little game got way too serious. Thank God it's over.

---

I didn't have the strength to argue. He was right and I knew it.

— What do we do with them? — he asked.

— Send them to our doctors. No noise. Nobody needs to know a couple of kids almost finished off the Champ.

— You're the boss. — He put his arm around my back to lift me up. — But, bro. Today went way too far.

I agreed.

---

The kids who'd come up to watch didn't make a sound.

Every month they watched the same thing — the two kids coming up, getting beat, going down. That month they saw the Champ leave the rooftop carried out, with a piece of his ear missing, and two little punks passed out being taken down the stairs on cabinet doors ripped off their hinges.

Nobody said anything. On the Hill, what gets seen and not talked about disappears faster than what nobody saw.

The doctors got there in twenty minutes. They took care of the two of them first. That was my order.

---

There's one thing that stayed from this story, and it's the only one that matters to whoever tells the legend later.

Those two kids never got the pleasure of seeing the Champ on his knees.

Jack was out cold since the first punch. Kim went down from the pistol-whip the exact second my knees touched the dirt, and I blacked out right after. There was nobody left awake to see it.

Officially, I've never lost a fight in my life.

Only I know that on that Monday morning, on a rooftop in Marélia, two nine-year-old kids put me on the ground. And that, if it hadn't been for a pistol-whip from behind, I wouldn't be here telling you this.
`;export{e as default};
